<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfredo Stroessner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/2006">2006</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfredo Stroessner was a Paraguayan general who seized power in 1954 and ruled as president until 1989, one of the longest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorships</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. Backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> Party and the army, his regime persecuted opponents and built the Itaipu dam with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. He was overthrown in a coup and died in exile in Brazil.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfredo-stroessner.jpg" alt="Alfredo Stroessner" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frank Scherschel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfredo_Stroessner_at_desk_(1cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alfredo Stroessner Matiauda was born in Encarnación on 3 November 1912. His father, Hugo Stroessner, was an accountant from Hof in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> who had emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> in the late 1890s; his mother, Heriberta Matiauda, was of Guaraní and Spanish criollo descent. He joined the Paraguayan army at the age of 16 and, after attending military school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-war">Chaco War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, reaching the rank of first lieutenant by its end.</p><p>In the 1947 Paraguayan Civil War he supported the Colorado Party and played an important role in its victory. Opposition parties had been banned from 1947. In 1951 he became commander-in-chief of the army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Stroessner objected to President Federico Chaves's plan to arm the national police and removed him in a coup on 4 May 1954. The National Assembly appointed Tomás Romero Pereira president, and he called special elections. Stroessner was the Colorado Party's nominee and won on 11 July 1954 as the only candidate. He took office on 15 August 1954 and was re-elected seven times, in 1958, 1963, 1968, 1973, 1978, 1983 and 1988, nearly always by margins above 80 percent. The constitution of 1967 permitted his re-election, and changes in 1977 effectively allowed indefinite rule.</p><p>Soon after taking office he placed the country under a state of siege, which allowed indefinite detention without trial and banned public meetings. It was renewed every 90 days until 1987, apart from a brief period in 1959, and in practice it applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a> only after 1970. He was a committed anti-communist who brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> into the World Anti-Communist League and justified repression as a defence of the country. He gave a free hand to the military and to the Minister of the Interior, Edgar Ynsfrán, and relied on Colorado Party militias to crush dissent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lieutenant General Andrés Rodríguez Pedotti, his confidant, seized power in a coup on 2 and 3 February 1989, and Stroessner went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> on 5 February. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasilia">Brasília</a> on 16 August 2006, aged 93. Puerto Presidente Stroessner, named for him, became Ciudad del Este in 1989. In 1992 the so-called Archives of Terror were found, documents showing that his government took part in Operation Condor, the anti-communist agreement for persecuting exiles.</p><p>His government exiled 20,814 Paraguayans. Around 425 to 500 people were forcibly disappeared, and an estimated 18,000 to 20,000 were tortured or otherwise abused. His 35 years in office were the longest of any South American ruler. His supporters, known as Stronistas, still hold that his was a time of security and stability.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfredo Stroessner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>General <b>Alfredo Stroessner</b>, in power from 1954 to 1989, governed through the army and the Colorado Party under a state of siege renewed every 90 days.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-paraguay">Republic of Paraguay · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the autocratic regime of <b>Alfredo Stroessner</b> his Colorado Party used Guarani to appeal to ordinary Paraguayans, though Stroessner himself never gave an address in it.</blockquote><a href="/language/guarani">Guarani · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Encarnación, in what is now <a href="/countries/paraguay">Paraguay</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasilia">Brasília</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-27.33,-55.87&amp;year=1973" aria-label="Encarnación on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3082 3166 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alfredo Stroessner"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3532 3436h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3532 3436h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-27.33,-55.87&amp;year=1973">What was here in 1973? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
